potentials based on an axisymmetric density model. ... Fluctuations in N-body potentials, which scale inversely with the square-root of N in these experiments, scatter stars between adjacent orbits; this speeds up mixing.\n\nセミナー : 宇宙 (英語)\n\n[更新日：2023/01/19 | 登録日：2022/10/27] 　\n\nMixing in Smooth and N-Body Potentials\n\nJoshua E. Barnes (ハワイ大学天文学研究所)\n\n2022/11/28 16:00 --\n京都大学基礎物理学研究所　研究棟・会議室 K206 & 〖Zoom〗\n\n要旨 :\nGalaxies evolve towards equilibrium by mixing. Phase mixing gradually disperses stars on time-scales inversely proportional to their spread in orbital period, while chaotic mixing can approach equilibrium on a Liapounov time-scale. The difference between these mechanisms is clear in potentials generated from smooth density distributions. Is it preserved when the potential is generated by a finite number of particles, or does the underlying chaos of N-body systems force all mixing to be chaotic? I address this question by comparing mixing in smooth and N-body potentials based on an axisymmetric density model. The distinction between phase and chaotic mixing is clear for N = 2^22 bodies. For smaller values of N, phase-mixing is accelerated and the distinction is less obvious. Fluctuations in N-body potentials, which scale inversely with the square-root of N in these experiments, scatter stars between adjacent orbits; this speeds up mixing. In time-dependent N-body potentials, stars diffuse in orbital energy, increasing their spread of orbital periods. To fully recover the behavior observed in smooth potentials, simulations with N >> 2^22 bodies may be required; at that point, it may be worth asking if real galaxies are better approximated by N-body potentials.\n＊It will be held in hybrid. ＊Note that buildings are locked.\n＊People other than YITP members, interested in attending the seminar should contact seminar_ap{AT}yukawa.kyoto-u.ac.jp--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPiecewise linear potentials for fal